course: english
lesson: 42
title: Building tension and suspense
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Suspense is the delicious worry of not knowing yet: the reader holds their breath because something might happen, and the writer's job is to make them wait.
explain: When the teacher reads "He waited. Silence. Then a creak.", the whole class freezes, because short sentences slow time down and make every second feel important, like the ticking before a game show answer.
explain: Showing everything at once kills it: "There was a monster. It ate the teacher" is over before we can be scared, and "The ghost appeared, chased them, and they escaped" squashes a whole chase into one breath.
explain: Writers hold back: a noise nobody can explain, a door that will not quite close, a question like "What was behind the door?", an ellipsis that trails off..., and a chapter that stops on a cliffhanger, just as the floor gives way.
explain: Sam from Boston knew the secret: when his mum called his ghost story "wicked scary" ("wicked" means very in New England), he said, "I didn't say what the noise was."
fact: Suspense is the feeling of waiting and worrying about what will happen next.
fact: Tension builds when the writer slows the action and holds back information.
fact: Short sentences and one-word sentences can slow a moment and make it feel tense.
fact: Revealing the danger too fast removes the suspense.
fact: Hints such as sounds, shadows and strange details make readers imagine the worst, which is often scarier than any description.
fact: A rhetorical question, such as "What was behind the door?", is asked for effect, not for an answer; it makes the reader wonder with the character.
fact: An ellipsis (...) can make the reader pause and wait.
fact: A cliffhanger ends a chapter or episode at a tense moment, so the reader must read on.
fact: Using "suddenly" again and again stops it being sudden; one surprise works better than ten.
fact: In Leeds and the Midlands "mardy" means sulky or grumpy, not scary; "dead" can mean very, as in "dead scary".
fact: "Sus" is short for suspicious; in New England "wicked" means very.
exercise: The teacher reads "He waited. Silence. Then a creak." Why does the class hold its breath? | Short sentences slow time and make us wait | The sentences are too short to understand | Creaks are the scariest sound that exists | The teacher read it in a whisper
exercise: Ava writes "There was a monster. It ate the teacher." What is the problem? | The scare comes at once, with no waiting | Monsters are not allowed in stories at school | It needs a much longer name for the monster | Teachers should never appear in any stories
exercise: Liam from Leeds says "Me story's well mardy, it's dead scary, Miss." What does "mardy" really mean? | Sulky or grumpy | Very frightening | Really exciting | Full of ghosts
exercise: The teacher says "Use an ellipsis to make the reader wait..." What is an ellipsis? | Three dots | Two dashes | One long line | A row of commas
exercise: Kai writes "The ghost appeared, chased them, and they escaped." What should he do? | Slow it down, step by step | Add more ghosts in the same line | Write it all in capital letters | Move it to the very first line
exercise: Year 6: "Use a rhetorical question." Which is one? | What was waiting behind the door? | Can I have a biscuit, please, Mum? | Where do you keep the plates? | What time does the bus come?
exercise: Josh, 9, says "The vibe in the old house is sus, bro." What does "sus" mean? | Suspicious | Super cool | Sunny and warm | Sudden
exercise: Why is Josh's feeling useful for a scary story? | Sensing something is wrong builds suspense | Sus stories always end happily | It means the house will be sold | The house is safe
exercise: The teacher says "End the chapter on a cliffhanger." Which ending is one? | The floor cracked, and Mia began to fall. | Mia went home and had tea with Gran. | Mia went to bed. | Everyone was safe at last, the end.
exercise: A Year 4 story says "Suddenly... Suddenly... Suddenly..." What happens to the word? | It stops feeling sudden | It gets scarier | It turns into a cliffhanger | It becomes a rhetorical question
exercise: Mum in Boston says "That ghost story's wicked scary, kid. How'd you do that?" Sam says "I didn't say what the noise was." What technique did Sam use? | Holding back information | Telling the reader everything | Using lots of long lists | Writing a happy ending
exercise: Which sentence builds the most tension? | Something scratched at the window. Once. Twice. | A cat scratched the window, then went away. | The window was scratched by a ginger cat. | A cat scratched.
exercise: What is suspense? | Waiting and worrying about what comes next | Knowing exactly what will happen next | A happy ending with no problems | A list of ghost facts
exercise: Why can a hint be scarier than a full description? | Readers imagine something worse | Hints are always longer to read | Full descriptions are not allowed | Hints use more adjectives
exercise: Which pair of sentences is better for a tense moment? | She stopped. Listened. | She stopped and then she listened for quite a while. | She stopped, and after that she listened carefully. | She was stopping and was listening too.
exercise: What does a cliffhanger make the reader do? | Read on to find out | Close the book at once | Skip to the very first page | Forget what happened before
test: Your mate goes "scary stories need loads of blood and gore or they're mid." ("Mid" means not very good.) What is the truth? | Waiting and hints scare readers more than gore | The more gore, the scarier the story | Stories are only scary if someone dies | Nothing can make a story scary
test: ★★★ Which version builds the most suspense about what is in the box? | The lid moved. Just a little. From the inside. | Inside the box was a small, friendly, sleepy kitten. | There was a kitten in the box, and it was really sweet. | The box had a kitten, a ball and a blanket in it.
test: In a cartoon, the villain explains his whole secret plan to the hero before doing anything. What does it do to the suspense? | It drains it, as nothing is left to guess | It makes the whole story much more tense and scary | It makes the villain far more mysterious than before | It turns the cartoon into a cliffhanger
test: What would happen if a TV series answered every mystery at the end of each episode? | Viewers would have less reason to come back | Viewers would watch even more episodes | Every episode would end on a cliffhanger | The series would become a quiz show
test: A lad from Newcastle says "That story's canny scary, man." ("Canny" means quite or very in the North East.) What probably made it scary? | The writer kept us waiting and guessing | The writer told us the whole ending first | The writer used no full stops | The writer only described the sky and the clouds
test: ★★★ "I opened the door. There was nothing there. Then I heard my name — from behind me." Which two techniques are used? | Short sentences and a held-back surprise | A long list and a rhetorical question | A happy ending and lots of adjectives | A fairy-tale opening and a long list of colours
test: Your sister, dead sarcastic: "Wow, a ghost appeared and then left. Terrifying." What is she hinting? | The ghost scene went by far too fast | Ghosts should never appear in stories | She is truly terrified now | The story needs more ghosts
test: A Gen Z cousin says "That ending had me screaming, I'm literally deceased." ("Deceased" here means it was so good she's joking that she died.) Which ending did she probably read? | The phone lit up: "I'm in your house." | And they all lived happily ever after. | The end, thank you for reading. | Then they went home and slept.
test: ★★★ Which sentence uses a rhetorical question for suspense? | Who had left the gate open, and why was it still swinging? | "Who wants pizza?" asked Dad, and all of us shouted yes at once. | What is the capital of France? Paris. | Where are my shoes? I asked Mum.
test: Why might a writer use a one-word sentence like "Silence."? | To slow the moment and make it stand out | Because the writer ran out of words | To show that the story has ended | To make the reader skip ahead
test: A girl in Texas says "I'm fixin' to write the scariest story y'all ever read." Which is the best first tip? | Don't show the monster too soon | Put the monster's name in the title | Explain the whole ending in line one | Use "suddenly" a lot
exam: Identify the technique whereby a chapter is concluded at a moment of unresolved danger. | A cliffhanger | A resolution | A simile | A topic sentence
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the effect of a sequence of short sentences in a tense passage? | It decelerates the action and heightens anticipation | It accelerates the action and diminishes anticipation | It replaces all description with dialogue | It signals the conclusion of the narrative
exam: Select the response consistent with established principles of suspense. | Information concerning the threat is withheld from the reader | All information is supplied to the reader at the outset | The threat is described fully in the first sentence | The narrative avoids all uncertainty
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the regional term "mardy" as employed in the North and Midlands of England. | Sulky or ill-tempered | Frightening or alarming | Courageous or bold | Exhausted or weary
